• **Real defect — Mach-O calls can still bypass exact stub-entry validation.**  
  `scripts/checks/lib/stack_bound.py:91` records `__stubs` as a stub *name*, and the fallback at `scripts/checks/lib/stack_bound.py:610-615` accepts any call whose symbolic base matches that name. Consequently, both an unmapped call to `<__stubs>` and a call into the middle of an entry are accepted without a corresponding `stub ADDR SYMBOL` line.

  Concrete accepted reproducer:

  ```text
  0000000000001000 <_main>:
      1000: str x30, [sp, #-0x10]!
      1004: bl 0x1044 <__stubs+0x4>
      1008: ldr x30, [sp], #0x10
      100c: ret

  Disassembly of section __TEXT,__stubs:

  0000000000001040 <__stubs>:
      1040: adrp x16, 0x2000
      1044: ldr x16, [x16]
      1048: br x16
  stub 0x0000000000001040 _write
  ```

  Running the analyzer on this input produces:

  ```text
  bound 16
  external __stubs 16
  ```

  Only `0x1040` is a mapped stub entry; the call targets `0x1044`. It skips the `adrp` that establishes `x16`, reaches the unanalysed `br x16`, and is nevertheless treated as a returning external call. Subsequent execution and stack accesses are therefore outside the claimed bound. The same fallback also accepts `bl 0x1040 <__stubs>` with no indirect-symbol lines at all, so the new unmapped-stub control does not cover this spelling.

  For Mach-O, external-call recognition should use only `stubs["addrs"]`; the section-name fallback must be restricted to ELF PLT handling. Add controls for both `<__stubs>` without a mapping and `<__stubs+0x4>` with only the entry address mapped.

  I found no separate unsound transition in the single-slot or link-register join changes: the tested deallocate/call/reallocate stale-slot restore was refused because its written-byte state had been cleared.

  VERDICT: REJECT

